## Test Anti-Pattern Audit — Severity-Ranked Findings

This test file is a textbook example of **"coverage without verification"**. Every production method is invoked (hence 95% coverage), but almost nothing meaningful is asserted. Here's the ranked breakdown:

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests That Prove Nothing

**1. Fire-and-forget (no assertion at all)**
Tests: `AddProduct_Works` (L19-21), `UpdatePrice_Works` (L34-35), `RemoveProduct_Works` (L39-41), `GetProductCount_Works` (L65-66), `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L70-74), `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L86-88)

**Anti-pattern:** "Executed ≠ Verified." These tests only check the method doesn't throw. A `Save()` that silently drops data would pass.

**Fix examples:**
```csharp
// UpdatePrice_Works → verify the new price persisted
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated!.Price);

// RemoveProduct_Works → verify the product is gone
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));

// ApplyBulkDiscount_Works → verify correct math
_catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);
Assert.AreEqual(8.991m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);

// ImportFromCsv_Works → verify the product was stored
_catalog.ImportFromCsv("SKU-001,Widget,9.99");
var p = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", p!.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, p.Price);
```

---

### 🟠 High — Assertions That Are Vacuously True

**2. `Assert.IsNotNull` on value types / guaranteed non-null references**
Tests: `GetProduct_Works` (L28), `SearchProducts_Works` (L51), `GetAllProducts_Works` (L59), `ExportToCsv_Works` (L81)

**Anti-pattern:** `List<T>` and `string` from these methods are never `null` — only empty. `IsNotNull` passes even if the results are completely wrong.

**Fix examples:**
```csharp
// GetProduct_Works → verify actual field values
var result = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", result!.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, result.Price);

// SearchProducts_Works → verify filtering actually works
var results = _catalog.Search("Widget");
Assert.AreEqual(1, results.Count);
Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001", results[0].Sku);

// GetAllProducts_Works → verify count
Assert.AreEqual(1, all.Count);

// ExportToCsv_Works → verify content
Assert.IsTrue(csv.Contains("SKU-001,Widget,9.99"));
```

